**Switches vs Routers:**
A switch is a device used to interconnect computers within the same local 
environment so they can communicate with each other. We use copper cable 
(RJ45) to connect computers to the switch. There are different types/
categories of these copper cables — CAT-5, CAT-6, etc. Fiber optic cables 
are for better and faster connections than copper cable — we always use 
cables to connect computers to a switch. However, a switch cannot use 
wireless technology. If you want the devices in the same environment to be 
connected to each other over wireless technology, we can use an "Access 
Point," which uses wireless with an antenna to communicate with equipment, 
unlike a switch device which uses cables to communicate with each other.

A switch device is enough for computers in the same LAN to communicate.
A router has no role in the communication of devices in the same LAN.

If PC1 wants to send a packet to the internet, the flow is:
PC1 → Switch (cables) → Router (cables) → Internet (ISP cables)

A router is a device we use to connect to another computer in a different 
network/world. The internet is a structure that connects all the LANs all 
over the world. A home router is a combo device that is a mixture of router 
and switch.

**Single Point of Failure:** If we give an entire load to a single point, 
and that point fails, everything connected to it fails too — that's why 
network design matters.

**Routing tables:** Each router must have a special table called a routing 
table. A routing table tells the router what route to send the packet 
through. The internet is the network of networks. Packet transmission is 
the basics of connecting to the internet or communicating with a computer 
on another side of the world.

**LAN (Local Area Network):** It's a group/collection of devices in one 
physical place such as a school, office, etc. NB: You can't create a LAN 
between computers located far away from each other. By using a switch 
device, we can create a Local Area Network.

Packet → Frame. If computer A can send a packet to computer B, this means 
both computers are on the same network. Flow: Sending device → Switch → 
Receiving device. The higher the number of ports on a switch, the higher 
the price. These ports are called LAN ports.

**WAN (Wide Area Network):** It's a network made up of the combination of 
numerous different LANs. Why do we need a WAN? Because the internet is a 
public network — it's public, but huge companies that have offices across 
the world still want to communicate between each computer in each office. 
Yes, they can use the internet, and without it they can't communicate. But 
that company has to create another network — a wider one — so different 
computers across the globe but the same company can communicate as if 
they're in the same place. That's when WAN comes in. But rather than it 
being a public network, it's rather a private network — only that company 
and its computers can have access to it.

**VPN:** A VPN tunnel creates a special network connection over a public 
network. WAN networks are built using VPN technology. We use a switch 
device to create a LAN, while we use a router to create a WAN. With the 
help of WAN, different devices act as if they are in the same environment.

**Internet backbone:** A network setup by global ISPs to be able to 
communicate with each other.

**ISP hierarchy:** Local ISPs connect neighborhoods, and regional ISPs 
connect cities in a country. In summary, the first step to connect to the 
internet is the local ISP, and if you examine your router, you can see a 
cable coming from your local ISP connected to it. Some local ISPs can 
connect to the global ISP without having to go through the regional ISP, 
and this is mostly due to a close infrastructure of the local ISP to the 
global ISP.

To access a website: the user sends a request message to the server, the 
server sends a response message back to the user.

**Peering:** It's the technique in which a server establishes an almost 
direct connection with the user. It's used by giant companies. Global ISPs 
are responsible for international communication.

