### My 9 self-quiz questions and answers:

**Q1: What is the difference between a switch and a router?**
A switch is used to interconnect computers in the same local environment 
with each other. A router is used to connect one computer to another 
computer, in a different environment. Illustration: computers connected 
together with a switch can share data and communicate with each other. BUT 
while doing all that they want, to have access to the internet, they need 
a router. Copper cables are used to connect one computer to another via a 
switch. But for these devices to have access to the internet, they need a 
router — a router is what connects to the ISP (via fiber optic cable/
copper cable), which gives the gateway for devices to use the internet.

**Q2: Why can 2 devices connect on the same LAN and communicate through a 
switch without using the router?**
These devices can communicate through a switch without using the router 
because the switch has ports where cables are physically connected from 
all computers in that area (may be an office, school). All computers 
connect to that switch, which receives data, processes it, and knows the 
destination which that specific data "packet" is bound for. If device A 
wants device B to receive a packet, all devices in the same location are 
connected. The switch receives the packet, processes it, knows where that 
packet is bound for, then sends it to device B. Though these 2 devices 
connected on the same LAN can communicate without a router via a switch, 
they won't have access to the internet.

**Q3: If my laptop wants to open www.google.com, describe the journey from 
my laptop until it reaches the google server.**
My laptop sends a packet/data/request to my home router. (A home router is 
made up of switch and router). The router receives it and sends it to the 
internet, which is made up of so many other routers. The packet chooses 
what path to take — the fastest and most convenient path. Then it sends it 
to the server where google.com is saved/kept. In return, google.com, after 
receiving that packet, checks and sees what exactly I requested, processes 
it, and sends it back through its server, which sends it back to the 
internet, then back to my own home router, then to my laptop — then I will 
be able to access that website. Also, if any google server is close, or my 
ISP to my router has good infrastructure, it won't have to send it to the 
global ISP — it can send it directly to the google server.

**Q4: If a company has offices in Abuja, Lagos, and London, would they use 
a LAN or a WAN to connect all the offices?**
They will use a WAN. Because for them to use a LAN to connect laptops in 
Abuja to laptops in London, a very long, inconvenient, and expensive 
process would be needed which won't be possible. So instead, they take the 
LAN of each office where these laptops are connected, and pass them through 
the internet, but on a private line which the company creates themselves 
and is only accessible to the company themselves — and all this is a Wide 
Area Network. Also remember, a Wide Area Network can't work without the 
internet.

**Q5: What is a host?**
A host is a device that can communicate with one another. Example: Phones, 
Laptops, Desktops.

**Q6: Your home router loses power. What devices in my house will still be 
able to communicate with each other? Will they still be able to access the 
internet?**
If my home router suddenly loses power, the devices that will still be able 
to communicate with each other are devices that have been set up to 
communicate using a switch. So when the router goes down, those devices 
can still communicate with each other but won't have access to the 
internet, because the router serves as the gateway to the internet — 
without it, devices can't have access to the internet.

**Q7: A router has a routing table. Why? What will happen if it doesn't 
have a routing table?**
A router has a routing table because it tells the data/packets which pass 
through the router what path to follow, so both endpoints receive these 
packets faster and more conveniently. Take it like a map — it tells a 
delivery driver ("the packet") what road to follow so it can get to the 
owner or receiver of that package faster and easier. Without it, those 
data/packets are lost and won't get to their destinations.

**Q8: A company wants employees in different countries to work as if they 
were in the same office. Why might they use a VPN?**
They might use a VPN to mask and protect data/packets from one endpoint to 
another. The internet is a public place — any skilled personnel can have 
access to that data. Since the company wants employees in different 
countries to work as if they were in the same office, they might use a VPN 
to encrypt any data both offices send and receive, to make it secure — 
remember, there's no 100% security on the internet. A VPN makes it harder 
and keeps this data encrypted so that only the router in each office can 
decrypt this data before sending it to each employee.

**Q9: Explain the difference between internet, router, switch, LAN, WAN, 
and IP address — using a football analogy.**
Imagine we are playing a football match, using a 4-5-1 formation as a 
scenario. Every position given is permanent — nobody leaves their position, 
they just stand and wait for instructions.

The internet is like the midfield — for team A to score on team B, they 
must pass the midfield; if not, they can never score on team B. So the 
internet is the midfield everyone has to pass through to reach the other 
side.

Each player can communicate with each other, and even say out their 
tactics openly, because they're on their own half of the field — take it 
as LAN. When they finally go to their opponent's half, they are far away 
but still need to communicate, but in a very private and secure way — still 
on the huge public field (for all players on both halves) but team A's 
players need to let other players back on their own half know what's about 
to happen — pass well, make the run, and all that. They set up a network 
where they can talk on the public field, but only team A players will 
understand it. That network is WAN — still public, but only accessible to 
team A.

